On Mon, 27 Dec 1993 nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu wrote:

> My best guess about the effect of parents being able to select genetic 
> traits for their children is that most parents in the US will 
> choose health for their children, but they won't especially choose
> intelligence, though they will select against retardation.
> 
> What I'm looking at is what sort of self-transformation people
> seem to want for themselves, and what they support in terms of
> schools. This suggests that what most people (in the US) want is 
> "good" looks and atheletic ability. (Anyone have similar guesses
> about what traits might be popular in other countries or cultures?)

I agree with this, and in fact suspect that most marriages (and quite likely
most failed marriages) are the result of this thinking (I may be wrong, but
then I often am).

The thing that this mind set believes is that if one has good looks and is
a good athelete, then they will make lot's of money in such things as
acting, modelling, and/or sports. This may or may not be true, and I suspect
is a myth. However, if large amounts of the population decided to go this
route, we may have an increase in the number of good looking, athletic
people, but probably no corresponding increase in the demand for them, so
we'll also have lot's of average intelligence, good looking, athletic,
welfare bums. Although I think an increase in the number of people with
average intelligence (whatever that means) could also be of great
benefit, and the health benefit would probably be enormous...

Those opting to include high intelligence as a top priority however are more
likely to bring forth the people who actually make great changes.

And if long life is also a priority, these individuals will also have more time
to bring about change and grow.

Of course this makes a great many assumptions about the interplay of
intelligence and productivity, long life and good health, etc. but I think
relates to the general way things would happen quite well.

> I'm still in favor of access to genetic engineering--and look forward
> to the day when people can choose for themselves (through tailored
> viruses or somesuch)--both because it's the only way that *I* can
> make personal use of g.e. and because the ethical problems of choosing 
> for one's children would become much smaller. 
> 
> Part of this is the opportunities for interesting changes that I
> see for myself, but a lot of it is a desire to see the intelligent
> life on this planet (eventually, it won't be a single "human race"
> in the sense that we use the word now) become much more wildly
> interesting and various. I'm not expecting to see optimization
> for intelligence or any other well-defined trait. 

Directed evolution is not something that (IMHO) can ever homogenize the human
race (or any species). The reproductive process if sufficiently random to
avoid this, and actually is a relatively Extropian process (operating on a
low level)... Diversity will always be with us. Even identical twins are
not completely alike in the people they are (unless of course we throw out
the window the idea of free will, which I hope all people have already done)

> Actually, if we're just talking about survival/ability to influence
> the future, maybe we should be talking about designing children
> to be good at influencing other people while being willing to
> take some losses on verbal/math intelligence if necessary, especially
> if there are still suckers out there who are willing to raise the
> technicians. 

Can't really say how much of this is genetic or the environment. I suspect
it has more to do with how a child is raised, and the self-image they have
of themselves. I think the single most important thing that should be taught
to a child is that they are an individual, and that they are important.
A positive self-image is an extremely useful thing. If more kid's were
raised to think for themselves, take responsibilty for action's as early as
possible, and to see themselves in a positive light, I believe we'd see
quite a lot of good things happening...

> It's all still evolution in action--it's just that genetic and
> memetic evolution become intertwined in interesting ways.

Yes, genetic engineering is only a part of the story. Having diversity is
also important in the ideas we have floating around, and having access to
that information is even more important. The coming era of global communication
which we are entering will bring about even more changes (is bringing about
more changes) than genetics has done in the past 10,000 years... in terms of
people's ideas...

> Nancy Lebovitz

