From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: Why have children?

>My best guess about the effect of parents being able to select genetic 
>traits for their children is that most parents in the US will 
>choose health for their children, but they won't especially choose
>intelligence, though they will select against retardation.

>Nancy Lebovitz

I have a little anecdotal evidence on the other side. A close friend of
ours is a very bright woman and a talented musician. She is also an adopted
child.  Her adoptive parents I would classify as successful blue collar.
She was very close to them, and is still very close to her mother (her
father is dead). On the one occasion when I visited her parents it was
clear, from photos of her on the walls and similar evidence, that they were
delighted to be ducks who had hatched a swan.

Someone else in this thread suggested that females would be more
pro-eugenic than males. I think that is a rather complicated issue.
Although a male can impregnate many women, the average male has the same
number of children as the average female (assuming equal numbers of males
and females, which is approximately true). So the male who knows that he
has only enough child-rearing resources to persuade one woman to bear his
children would like (genetically speaking) to spend his resources on
children who combine good genes with his genes. Only the male who is
sufficiently desirable so that females are willing to bear his children
without receiving any significant support in rearing them can follow the
strategy of impregnating any willing female.

What is true is that women can afford to be more eugenic in their choice.
Almost any female can choose to have her children by a very high quality
male, provided she does not expect him to support them; in some species the
result is that only a small percentage of the males reproduce. Males do not
have a similar option, since it is womb space that is the scarce
reproductive resource.

