debates have statements of personal belief or emotion. They're not
persuasive by themselves, that's for sure. All I'm saying here is that
in the _informal_ debates we have here, mentions of revulsion or
concern seem to add some zest to the debate. In contrast, speculating
about the motives of one's opponent--that she wants Jews put in ovens,
or that he must've been a nerd in college--seem out of line. Purely ad
hominem.)

I'm looking forward to being further buffeted by these debates. And
that's a welcome thing.

Next move is Romana's.

