As I pointed out, it's hard not to be "stupid enough" to provide the 
hospital with a sample that contains your unique genetic signature.  
If you withold your medical history, your medical service might be
quite degraded, or service might be refused.  Tissue or blood
samples are taken in practically all non-trivial medical procedures.
Genetic signatures are far more reliable for tracing you than your social 
security number, name, etc., both for medical insurance and general 
credit tracking purposes.  Such signatures will likely replace numbers 
and names in commercial credit and government databases.  DNA signatures 
are already augementing fingerprints as tracing evidence in criminal
investigations.

Furthermore, we need to talk about the incentive of the hospital,
and the hospital's employees, to protect their patients' privacy.
Opportunities to untraceably leak patient info will be quite
lucrative to the world's medical insurers.  The patient is effectively 
helpless; you can't encrypt your genes.

> The service would get in touch (somehow) with
> the people on it's list notifying them that they are on a list, and can
> avoid the release of information by paying more money. 

This isn't much different from blackmail.  The list owner can
charge more to protect information on controversial medical services,
and services that medical insurers would like to know about.
Then, mysteriously, the information might leak anyway, if one of
the list owners or their employees sell it untraceably.  

Notification is also an expensive infrastructure to build; it's much
cheaper to just give out the information and not notify the
person it refers to.  For this reason very little notification
is done in today's info economy.

There is a niche here for a hospital rating agency to "ping"
hospitals by themselves tracking a group of patients,
then posing as an insurance company to try to buy the information.
We might see "privacy ratings" of hospitals if leakage and
blackmail become a major problem.  These ratings can only
be advisory.  They don't solve the problem of the lab technician 
selling genetic info under the table, but lab security might mitigate 
that problem.

It takes money to solve information leakage problems, money
that could have gone to the medical procedures themselves. 
Furthermore, witholding the information carries severe insurance 
penalties.  All in all, medical privacy may not be worth the effort.

