I would suggest that having only two adults being responsible for each
child is too skimpy and fragile. Firstly, there's obviously not a
lot of redundancy in the system. If one adult gets seriously ill or
dies or leaves, the children are suddenly in much worse condition.

Secondly, having children with only one other adult to help seems
to overload many people. Do you have some positive reason for not
wanting more adults (whether blood relations or not) in the network?

I've speculated on whether it would work for the big adult commitment
to be co-parenting rather than marriage. 

Also, it might make sense for parent to take it as normal to choose
successor parents for their children if they (the first set of
parents) are incapacitated. 

